@dc:creator "SIE Disruption Lab"
@dc:contributor "Joe Gregory"
@dc:description "A vocabulary capturing the Modes and Functions ontological pattern for spacecraft systems engineering. A Mode is a kind of State — a Disposition specifically dependent on a System — that determines which Functions are available for execution. A FunctionalChain is a specification (PrescriptiveICE) of an ordered sequence of function invocations; validity is checked structurally against the mode-function availability model, with no reference to runtime state. State machine transitions (Transition instances, already defined in UAOS_States as a subclass of Function) carry their availability in the mode they exit, derived by the TransitionAvailability rule. The HasSubTransition relation captures the hierarchical trigger requirement for reconfiguring lower-level elements during a mode transition — formalized here with reference to ECSS-E-ST-70-11C Table 5.2 Step 1b, but applicable wherever spacecraft or complex system behavior is organized around discrete operational states with hierarchical reconfiguration requirements. The vocabulary is standard-agnostic in structure: ECSS provides terminological grounding, not a constraint on applicability."
@dc:hasVersion "0.2"
vocabulary <http://uaontologies.com/UAOS_Domain/UAOS_Modes#> as modes {

	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

	/////// Vocabulary Imports ///////

	extends <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> as dc
	extends <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> as rdfs
	extends <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> as xsd

	extends <http://uaontologies.com/UAOS_TopLevel/UAOS_TopLevel#> as tlo
	extends <http://uaontologies.com/UAOS_Core/UAOS_Information#> as info
	extends <http://uaontologies.com/UAOS_Domain/UAOS_States#> as States
	extends <http://uaontologies.com/UAOS_Domain/UAOS_SystemArchitecture#> as sa

	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////


	/////// Vocabulary Definitions ///////


	///// Concepts

	@rdfs:label "Mode"
	@dc:description "A [=Mode=] is a kind of State that is specifically dependent on a System at a given level of the logical architecture (spacecraft, subsystem, or assembly). A Mode determines which Functions are available for execution by that System: only Functions declared as availableIn that Mode may be performed while the System occupies it. This formalises the ECSS-E-ST-70-11C definition: 'the operational state of a spacecraft, subsystem or payload in which certain functions can be performed'. Note the distinction between Mode (system/subsystem/assembly level) and State (component level): components have States whose values are determined by the Mode of the parent assembly. Mode is modelled as a Disposition rather than a role or quality because, like all Dispositions, it inheres in a specific bearer (the System), is persistent (it holds until explicitly changed by a Transition), and is realisable (it is realised whenever the System is in that operational configuration)."
	concept Mode < States:State [
		restricts some tlo:sdo to sa:System
		restricts all tlo:hasContinuantPart to Mode
		restricts all tlo:isContinuantPartOf to Mode
	]

	@rdfs:label "FunctionalChain"
	@dc:description "A [=FunctionalChain=] is a PrescriptiveICE — a specification prescribing an ordered sequence of function invocations to be performed by specific Systems. It corresponds to an On-Board Control Procedure (OBCP) as defined in ECSS-E-ST-70-11C. A FunctionalChain is a specification, not an execution: it exists independently of whether it has been or will be executed. Validity is assessed structurally: a FunctionalChain is valid if, for every FunctionalChainStep it contains, the specified Function is available in at least one Mode of the specified executing System. Steps that specify a Function not available in any Mode of the executor represent a structural error — the function can never be performed by that system regardless of its current mode."
	concept FunctionalChain < info:PrescriptiveICE

	@rdfs:label "FunctionalChainStep"
	@dc:description "A [=FunctionalChainStep=] is a PrescriptiveICE that forms a single ordered step within a FunctionalChain. Each step specifies: (1) the Function to be performed, via specifiesFunction; (2) the System that should perform it, via specifiesExecutor; and (3) its position in the execution sequence, via hasSequencePosition. A FunctionalChainStep is a specification — it is not a FunctionOccurrence. Validity checking operates entirely at the disposition and specification level: the question is whether the specified Function is available in any Mode of the specified System, not whether the Function has been or is being executed."
	concept FunctionalChainStep < info:PrescriptiveICE


	///// Relations

	@rdfs:label "AvailableIn"
	@dc:description "[=AvailableIn=] relates a Function to a Mode in which it is available for execution. A Function may be available in multiple Modes. For non-transition Functions this relation must be explicitly asserted by the modeller. For Transition instances (subclass of Function via UAOS_States), availableIn is automatically inferred by the TransitionAvailability rule from the state machine structure: if a Transition exits a Mode, it is available in that Mode. This unifies the state machine and the availability model under a single relation, with the state machine serving as the authoritative source for transition availability."
	relation entity AvailableIn [
		from tlo:Function
		to Mode
		@rdfs:label "available in"
		forward availableIn
		@rdfs:label "makes available"
		reverse makesAvailable
		asymmetric
		irreflexive
	]

	@rdfs:label "HasSubTransition"
	@dc:description "[=HasSubTransition=] relates a parent-level Transition to a child-level Transition that must execute as part of the parent transition. This captures ECSS-E-ST-70-11C Table 5.2 Step 1b (Reconfiguring Lower Levels): when a System transitions to a new Mode, all lower-level elements it contains must also transition to their corresponding Modes, and each such child transition is a HasSubTransition of the parent. The completeness of the HasSubTransition hierarchy — specifically, that every parent Transition has a declared sub-transition for every child System — is the key structural invariant checked by Query 02. If a parent Transition lacks a sub-transition for some child System, the mode transition cannot complete, regardless of which mode the system is currently in."
	relation entity HasSubTransition [
		from States:Transition
		to States:Transition
		@rdfs:label "has sub-transition"
		forward hasSubTransition
		@rdfs:label "is sub-transition of"
		reverse isSubTransitionOf
		asymmetric
		irreflexive
	]

	@rdfs:label "HasStep"
	@dc:description "[=HasStep=] relates a FunctionalChain to its constituent FunctionalChainSteps. The set of steps, together with their hasSequencePosition values, fully defines the ordered specification of the chain."
	relation entity HasStep [
		from FunctionalChain
		to FunctionalChainStep
		@rdfs:label "has step"
		forward hasStep
		@rdfs:label "is step of"
		reverse isStepOf
		asymmetric
		irreflexive
	]

	@rdfs:label "SpecifiesFunction"
	@dc:description "[=SpecifiesFunction=] relates a FunctionalChainStep to the Function it specifies should be performed. Because this relation targets a Function (a Disposition), not a FunctionOccurrence (a Process), the specification is mode-checkable: validity reduces to asking whether the specified Function is available in at least one Mode of the specified executor."
	relation entity SpecifiesFunction [
		from FunctionalChainStep
		to tlo:Function
		@rdfs:label "specifies function"
		forward specifiesFunction
		@rdfs:label "is function specified by"
		reverse isFunctionSpecifiedBy
		asymmetric
		irreflexive
	]

	@rdfs:label "SpecifiesExecutor"
	@dc:description "[=SpecifiesExecutor=] relates a FunctionalChainStep to the System that the step specifies should perform the Function. Combined with SpecifiesFunction and the AvailableIn relation, this enables structural validation: for a step to be structurally valid, the specified Function must be available in at least one Mode of the specified System."
	relation entity SpecifiesExecutor [
		from FunctionalChainStep
		to sa:System
		@rdfs:label "specifies executor"
		forward specifiesExecutor
		@rdfs:label "is executor specified by"
		reverse isExecutorSpecifiedBy
		asymmetric
		irreflexive
	]


	///// Scalar Properties

	@rdfs:label "has sequence position"
	@dc:description "[=hasSequencePosition=] assigns an integer execution position to a FunctionalChainStep within its parent FunctionalChain. Position 1 is the first step to be executed; steps are executed in ascending order of position."
	scalar property hasSequencePosition [
		domain FunctionalChainStep
		range xsd:integer
	]


	///// Rules

	@rdfs:comment "TRANSITION AVAILABILITY RULE: A Transition t is available in Mode m if t exits m. This rule derives availableIn for all Transitions from the state machine structure alone, making the state machine the single source of truth for transition availability. Non-transition Functions require explicit availableIn assertions. This rule, together with the HasSubTransition relation, enables structural checking of hierarchical mode transition completeness via SPARQL without any runtime state assertions."
	rule TransitionAvailability [
		States:exits(t, m) -> availableIn(t, m)
	]

}
